'''Socionics''' is a theoretical typology of personality and interpersonal relations associated with Aušra Augustinavičiūtė. It draws on Jungian psychological types and Kępiński's information-metabolism concept. The existence of a structured model and a literature describing it must be distinguished from psychometric validation of the model's claims.

== Theory and historical development ==
Pietrak's review (published in volume 47 of ''Cognitive Systems Research'', 2018) describes the model's intellectual origins and its intended use in explaining interpersonal communication. This is a theoretical review, not a large independent prospective validation of the entire typology. Socionics borrows the idea of information metabolism but introduces its own typological structure and predictions. The psychiatric model does not imply those predictions.

== Model A and the proposed type structure ==
Model A arranges eight information elements in eight positions, commonly grouped into four pairs called Ego, Super-Ego, Super-Id and Id. The positions are assigned different theoretical roles and the sixteen types differ in which elements occupy them. The Socionics Association of Japan's Model A description is useful as a practitioner source for the internal nomenclature, not as evidence that the proposed positions correspond to empirically isolated neural or psychological mechanisms.

A conventional typology uses four binary distinctions, giving <math>2^4=16</math> combinations if every combination is permitted. This combinatorial result explains how sixteen labels can arise; it does not prove that the population forms sixteen natural clusters. Nor are the labels necessarily interchangeable with those of the Myers–Briggs Type Indicator, despite shared Jungian ancestry and some similar terminology.

== Empirical questions and validation design ==
Separate at least four hypotheses:

* '''Measurement reliability:''' independent trained raters or repeated administrations should assign reproducible types under specified conditions.
* '''Structural validity:''' observed data should support the proposed categorical structure rather than a competing continuous-trait or alternative latent model.
* '''Predictive validity:''' type assignments should predict previously unseen outcomes beyond relevant covariates and baseline models.
* '''Intertype relations:''' predefined pairwise predictions should predict interaction outcomes beyond individual trait scores, context and relationship history.

An illustrative test of classification reliability for two raters uses Cohen's kappa, <math>\kappa=(p_o-p_e)/(1-p_e)</math>, where <math>p_o</math> is observed agreement and <math>p_e</math> is the agreement expected from the raters' marginal distributions. Report confidence intervals, the full confusion matrix and category prevalences; high agreement alone does not establish validity. Type assignments should be made independently of the outcome used to test predictions.

For a prospective intertype study, preregister the classification protocol and predictions, recruit an appropriately sampled cohort, blind outcome assessors, account for repeated observations and dyadic dependence, and compare held-out performance against dimensional-trait and context-only baselines. Testing many type pairs without multiplicity control or changing classifications after outcomes are known creates substantial opportunities for apparent confirmation. This is a proposed validation protocol, not an assertion that socionics has passed it.

== Competing evidence and limits ==
The AERA/APA/NCME testing standards require evidence appropriate to intended score interpretation and use. A broad taxometric meta-analysis (2020 online publication) reports considerably more support for dimensional than categorical representations across the personality and psychopathology literature. That result motivates competing dimensional models; it is not a direct test or definitive refutation of every socionics construct.

The available theoretical sources describe the system and its intended explanatory reach. They do not establish a validated sixteen-category latent structure, a universal map of compatibility, a diagnosis of mental disorder or a mapping to artificial-agent architectures. For practical decisions, the burden of evidence lies with the specific instrument and application. An AI agent can be programmed to reproduce the typology's rules, but computational consistency would not validate the psychological theory.

